.TH CDATE 1 "2026-10-12" "compact-date-tools 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cdate \- encode a calendar date as three compact characters
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cdate
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RI [ "DATE OR TIMESTAMP ..." ]
.br
.B cdate
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.BI \-\-date " STRING"
.br
.B cdate
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.BI \-\-reference " FILE"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cdate
stores a Gregorian calendar year, month, and day in three case-sensitive
characters. For example,
.B A1B
means 2010-01-11: A is 2010, 1 is January, and B is day 11.
With no input, encode the current day in the output zone.
The output zone defaults to UTC.
.PP
Input and output zones are independent. Unzoned input defaults to UTC.
An input-zone option supplies a missing zone; an explicit offset determines
the instant itself. An output-zone option selects the calendar day to encode.
.SH COMPACT FORMAT
Each position indexes the alphabet
.BR 0123456789ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZabcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz .
The year index is the year minus 2000. The month and day indexes are their
ordinary calendar numbers.
.PP
Years 2000-2009 use 0-9, 2010-2035 use A-Z, and 2036-2061 use a-z.
Months 1-9 use 1-9; months 10-12 use A-C.
Days 1-9 use 1-9; days 10-31 use A-V.
The supported range is 2000-01-01 (011) through 2061-12-31 (zCV).
There are 22,646 valid codes.
.PP
Calendar validity is required. Month or day zero, February 30, and February 29
in a non-leap year are invalid. The final day in the output zone must fit the
supported range. The code stores no time, offset, or original zone.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h , " " \-\-help
Print usage and exit successfully without operational side effects.
.TP
.BR \-V , " " \-\-version
Print the command version and exit successfully.
.TP
.BR \-d , " " \-\-debug
Create a private execution log in the current directory.
.TP
.BI \-\-date " STRING"
Use one date expression instead of positional input. Positional words are
otherwise joined with spaces.
.TP
.BR \-i " " FORMAT ", " \-\-input\-format " " FORMAT
Select auto, ymd, mdy, dmy, unix, unix-ms, date, or javascript.
The default is auto. An explicit format requires a date input.
.TP
.BR \-r " " FILE ", " \-\-reference " " FILE
Encode the file's last modification instant in the output zone.
The file contents are not read. Cannot be combined with a date input.
.TP
.BR \-u , " " \-\-utc , " " \-\-universal
Encode the day in UTC. This is the output default.
.TP
.BR \-l , " " \-\-local
Encode the day in the inherited system or TZ local zone.
This changes output only.
.TP
.BR \-z " " ZONE ", " \-\-timezone " " ZONE
Encode the day in a named IANA zone. This changes output only.
.TP
.B \-\-input\-utc
Interpret unzoned input in UTC. This is the input default.
.TP
.B \-\-input\-local
Interpret unzoned input in the inherited system or TZ local zone.
.TP
.BI \-\-input\-timezone " ZONE"
Interpret unzoned input in a named IANA zone.
.PP
Choose at most one option in each zone group. Input and output options can be
combined. Duplicate singleton options, including aliases, are errors.
Use
.B \-\-
before a positional input beginning with a hyphen, or use an attached option
value such as
.BR \-\-date=\-1 .
.SH INPUT FORMATS
.SS Automatic detection
Recognized forms include ISO dates and date-times, eight-digit YYYYMMDD,
English month names, RFC email dates, standard date output, standard English
JavaScript Date strings, and ordinary numeric locale date/time strings.
Bare ISO years and year-months mean the first day of that period.
Unzoned calendar dates mean midnight in the input zone.
.PP
Numeric dates with the year last use month/day/year unless the first field
exceeds 12, in which case they use day/month/year. Select mdy or dmy to resolve
ambiguity. Numeric two-digit years use 00-68 for 2000-2068 and 69-99 for
1969-1999; output range checks still apply.
.PP
A leading @ means Unix seconds. Ten-digit values mean Unix seconds and
thirteen-digit values mean Unix milliseconds; fractions are allowed.
Other expressions, including relative dates, go to the installed date parser.
Time-only strings use the current day in the input zone.
.SS Explicit formats
ymd, mdy, and dmy require numeric calendar input, optionally followed by a clock
and zone. unix accepts seconds with up to nine fractional digits.
unix-ms accepts milliseconds with up to six fractional digits.
Epoch arithmetic is exact; excess precision, scientific notation, and trailing
junk are errors. Use explicit epoch modes for other digit counts.
.PP
date passes the input grammar directly to the installed date command,
bypassing automatic numeric interpretation.
.PP
javascript applies JavaScript date semantics after independent validation.
ISO date-only strings remain UTC even with an input-zone option.
Unzoned date-times use the input zone. Precision is milliseconds.
The TypeScript implementation uses native Date.parse; Rust implements standard
ISO and English strings without a JavaScript runtime. Nonstandard legacy
JavaScript parsing can differ; use ISO input for interchange.
.SS Validation and limits
Valid ISO 24:00 with zero minutes, seconds, and fraction means the next day's
midnight. Other hour-24 clocks, leap seconds, impossible calendars, invalid
numeric offsets, and skipped unzoned clock times are rejected.
For repeated times at a DST fall-back, the system parser chooses an occurrence;
use an explicit offset when the instant matters.
.PP
Arbitrary translated month names, non-Gregorian calendars, alternate digit
systems, and custom formats cannot be reliably autodetected.
Prefer ISO output, or select a numeric format. System parser grammar and
relative-date behavior depend on the installed date implementation.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cdate 2010-01-11
cdate 20100111
cdate January 11, 2010
cdate 2010-01-11T13:41:54Z
.fi
All print A1B.
.PP
.nf
cdate \-i dmy 11/01/2010
cdate \-i unix-ms 1263217314000
.fi
Both print A1B in UTC.
.PP
.nf
TZ=America/Los_Angeles cdate \-\-local 2010-01-11
.fi
Prints A1A: UTC midnight falls on January 10 locally.
.PP
.nf
TZ=America/Los_Angeles cdate \-\-input-local \-\-local 2010-01-11
.fi
Prints A1B: input and output both use the local zone.
.PP
.nf
cdate \-\-input-timezone Asia/Tokyo 2010-01-11
.fi
Prints A1A: Tokyo midnight is January 10 at 15:00 UTC.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PATH
Locates the GNU-compatible date command. TypeScript also requires Node.js.
.TP
.B TZ
Supplies the local zone when an input or output local option is selected.
It also determines the debug-log filename timestamp.
.TP
.BR LC_ALL , " " LC_TIME , " " LANG
Inherited by date. Localized parser support depends on that implementation.
.TP
.B TZDIR
Time-zone data directory when supported by the installed date implementation.
Rust also uses it to validate named zones; the default is /usr/share/zoneinfo.
.SH OUTPUT AND EXIT STATUS
Success prints one code and a newline and exits 0.
Invalid input, unrepresentable output, argument errors, file errors, parser
failures, timeouts, or logging failures exit 1 with an error on stderr.
Ordinary errors produce no compact result. Callers must check exit status:
a late output or log-close failure can occur after the result was written.
.SH FILES
.B _YYYYMMDD_HHMMSS_cdate.log
is created in the current directory by debug mode. Its timestamp uses local
time independently of input and output flags. Renaming the executable changes
the basename in the log filename. Files are created exclusively with mode
0600; a same-second collision fails without overwriting an existing log.
.PP
Logs record arguments, paths, zone choices, parsing, validation, and errors.
They may contain private input. Argument parsing happens before a log is
opened. Help and version never create one, even with debug selected.
.SH NOTES
Both implementations require a GNU-compatible date; BSD date is insufficient.
Each date call has a five-second timeout and a one-MiB limit per output pipe.
Arguments are passed without shell interpolation. No clock-setting flags or
network requests are used.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ddate (1),
.BR date (1)
